<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted, deliberately.

  Android 9 (API 28) blocks cleartext by default, and that default is right for an app that
  talks to one known production host over TLS. This app talks to a server the *user* runs, at
  an address the user types in — `http://10.0.2.2:8080` from an emulator, `http://192.168.1.x`
  from a phone on the same Wi-Fi. Neither has a certificate and neither can get one. Blocking
  cleartext here would not make those deployments secure, it would make them impossible, and
  the app would ship unable to do the only thing it does.

  What this costs, stated plainly: on a LAN, the JWT and every snapshot travel unencrypted and
  anyone on that network can read them. The trade is defensible for a self-hosted tool on a
  home network. It is not defensible over the open internet.

  If you expose your server publicly, put TLS in front of it and tighten this file to:

      <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

  which is a one-line change and costs nothing on a deployment that already has HTTPS.
  See docs/12-android-app.md for the reasoning and docs/09-deployment.md for the TLS work
  that is already on the production-readiness checklist.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <!--
              User-installed CAs are trusted so a self-hosted server behind a private CA — or
              a mitmproxy session while debugging the API — works without a custom build.
              Android stopped trusting these by default in API 24.
            -->
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
